INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (23, 23, 1, 8, 'Oktadian Adi Nugraha', 'Survey', 'Completed', 'complete', 'Rede Surabaya, East Java', '2024-03-22', '2024-03-31', '<div style="text-align: justify;"><span style="font-size: 0.875rem;">The plan maintenance system should be added with time bar for each item. The leakage at port winch anchor hydraulic pump should be immediately repaired. Insulating mats should be provided on each swicth board panel. Cargo securing book and manufacturer manual for engine should be provided onboard.</span></div>', '<div style="text-align: justify;"><span style="font-size: 0.875rem;">The structural condition of the vessel was generally in good condition, with good painting work and spot indentation sighted at several places. The port and starboard side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible. Generally, the deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop deck was found in fair condition. The emergency fire hose test was available for our inspection and found in satisfactory condition.rnrnThe navigational and communication equipment was sufficient for domestic local trading areas within Indonesian waters. Also, the machinery equipment was found in fair and satisfactory condition during our inspection.rnrnNevertheless, the crew''s skill in working and operating the vessel was considered sufficient, and they were familiar with the type and size of the vessel.</span></div>', '<div style="text-align: justify;"><div>SUMMARY</div><div><br></div><div>The survey onboard MV "SAMUDRA PRIMA 1" was carried out on 22 March 2024 at Rede Surabaya, East Java, with attendance:</div><div><br></div><div>1.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Iksyam - Master of the vessel</div><div>2.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Nasdi - Chief Officer of the vessel</div><div>3.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Rovyan - Second Officer of the vessel</div><div>4.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Martin Luther Sianressi - Chief Engineer of the vessel</div><div>5.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Yusmanto - Second Engineer of the vessel</div><div>6.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Asep Kurniawan - Third Engineer of the vessel</div><div><br></div><div>MV "SAMUDRA PRIMA 1" is a 1,486 GT general cargo vessel presently owned and operated by PT. Samudra Raya Indo Lines. At the time of our survey, the vessel was rendered as the container carrier between Kendari Port, Southeast Sulawesi and Surabaya Port, East Java round trip.</div><div><br></div><div>VESSEL CERTIFICATION AND DOCUMENTATION</div><div><br></div><div>Class survey records for hull and machinery were made available at the time of the survey. The class is maintained. In the meantime, the statutory certificate was in valid condition and not overdue from the next inspection schedule or renewal.</div><div><br></div><div>The latest UT thickness measurement report was made available and is in our possession after we disembarked from the vessel. The vessel''s steel structures'' diminution level is still below the class maximum allowable limit of 20%.</div><div><br></div><div>GENERAL CONDITION OF HULL AND DECK</div><div><br></div><div>As far as could be ascertained, the port and starboard side shell plating from aft to fore-ends, from sheer down to the waterline, was visually found in fair condition, and fair painting works with light indentation were sighted in places. The port and starboard side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible.</div><div><br></div><div>At our attendance, the visible condition of the deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop deck was found in fair condition. The port and starboard bulwark and railing on the forecastle, main, poop, and accommodation deck were found in order.</div><div><br></div><div>The mooring equipment, which is double bollard, fairlead, and roller, was found in fair condition, but the safe working load information was not marked. However, several fairleads at aft were found chipped. Most air vents and sounding pipes were also not clearly marked with the compartment they serve. The watertight door and booby hatch leading to the engine room were found in fair condition with a good rubber seal.</div><div><br></div><div>The winch and windlass are generally in fair condition and good operation. However, the hydraulic pump of port side winch anchor was found oil drain during tested. Therefore, the mooring work area was slippery with the sign of oil puddle on the forecastle deck.</div><div><br></div><div>At the time of our survey, the hatch cover pontoon was not applicable since the vessel was loaded with container. The internal condition of the cargo space was found clean and dry while the cargo hold steel structure including the frames and toe bracket was generally found in fair condition and well-coated. The suction test using the portable submersible pump on the cargo hold bilges was carried out at the time of our survey, and it was found in order when tested.Â </div><div><br></div><div>The inspected ballast tanks No. 4P, 4S, 5P, 5S, 6P and 6S were found in fair condition and free from significant wastage, pitting, and scale. The zinc anode was sighted on the inspected ballast tanks.</div><div><br></div><div>BRIDGE AND NAVIGATION</div><div><br></div><div>The navigation and communication equipment were in good condition when tested and considered sufficient for domestic or local trading within Indonesian waters.Â </div><div><br></div><div>The updated NTM (Indonesian Notice to Mariners) was available onboard. The nautical publications and charts were correctly updated. Meanwhile, the deck logbook was filled out properly by the crew.</div><div><br></div><div>The changeover instructions for emergency steering and blackout procedure were available and posted on the bridge.</div><div><br></div><div>MACHINERY SPACE AND STEERING GEAR</div><div><br></div><div>The vessel has 1 (one) main engine, 1 (one) auxiliary engine as registered on the class certificate, and 2 (two) generator sets. The Chief Engineer verbally advised that several machinery equipment testing records were available onboard. Meanwhile, the Planned Maintenance System (PMS) was available but was not completed with the scheduled maintenance date. However, the engine manufacturer manual and service bulletins were not available onboard.</div><div><br></div><div>The Chief Engineer verbally advised that the purifier and boiler had been dismantled from the vessel due to the vessel currently using the HSD (High-Speed Diesel) fuel engine. The insulation mats were not available on several switchboard panels. The engine logbook was sighted and found to be regularly updated.</div><div><br></div><div>The engine room bilges were found wet with oily water.Â </div><div><br></div><div>The emergency steering gear and generator were in good order when tested and provided with emergency procedures. The test record was also available onboard.Â </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>SAFETYÂ </div><div><br></div><div>The safety equipment was found to be in order. Sufficient numbers of life jackets were provided on board. 1 (one) lifeboat and 2 (two) life rafts were available on board. Portable fire extinguishers were in good condition and regularly serviced. The emergency fire pump test was available for our inspection and found in satisfactory condition.</div><div><br></div><div>The bunkering connection was found in good condition, and save-all was installed in the surrounding area. However, most air vents and sounding pipes were not clearly marked with the compartment they serve.</div><div><br></div><div>== END ==</div></div>', '2024-02-29', '2025-03-01', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (31, 28, 1, 8, 'Oktadian Adi Nugraha', 'Survey', 'Outstanding', 'complete', 'Agats Port, Asmat, Papua', '2024-07-04', '2024-06-30', '<p>The planned maintenance system should be maintained and carried out regularly according to the scheduled time frame. The corroded starboard side stairway on the main deck leading to the poop deck should be repaired and reinstate to its original condition. Insulating mats should be provided for each switchboard panel. A stability booklet should be provided onboard.<br></p>', '<p>The structural condition of the vessel was generally good, with adequate painting work and light indentations observed in several places. The port side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible. The deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop deck was generally in fair condition. The emergency fire hose test was available for our inspection and found to be in satisfactory condition.</p><p>The navigational and communication equipment was sufficient for domestic local trading areas within Indonesian waters. Additionally, the machinery equipment was found to be in fair and satisfactory condition during our inspection.</p><p>The crew''s skill in working and operating the vessel was considered sufficient, and they were familiar with the type and size of the vessel.</p>', '<p>SUMMARY</p><p><br></p><p>The survey onboard MV "DORAEMON" was carried out on 03 - 04 July 2024 at Agats Port, Asmat, Papua, with attendance:</p><p><br></p><p>1.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Suripto - Master of the vessel</p><p>2.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Sukowandono - Chief Officer of the vessel</p><p>3.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>M. Fuad - Second Officer of the vessel</p><p>4.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Yulius Anggoro - Chief Engineer of the vessel</p><p>5.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Giran - Second Engineer of the vessel</p><p>6.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Arrahman - Third Engineer of the vessel</p><p><br></p><p>MV "DORAEMON" is a 760 GT general cargo vessel previously owned and operated by PT Tirta Sarana Indo Lines. However, the vessel has just been handed over to the new owner, PT Sri Mutiara Alam. At the time of our survey, the vessel was carrying onboard building materials, which were mostly delivered from Java Island to Eastern Indonesia.</p><p><br></p><p>VESSEL CERTIFICATION AND DOCUMENTATION</p><p><br></p><p>Class survey records for the hull and machinery were made available at the time of the survey, and the class is maintained. Meanwhile, the statutory certificate was valid and not overdue for the next inspection or renewal.</p><p><br></p><p>The latest UT thickness measurement report was made available and is in our possession at the time of our survey. The diminution level of the vessel''s steel structures is still below the class maximum allowable limit of 20%.</p><p><br></p><p>GENERAL CONDITION OF HULL AND DECK</p><p><br></p><p>At the time of our survey, the vessel was berthed on the port side position at Agats Port. As far as could be ascertained, the port side shell plating from aft to fore-ends, from the sheer down to the waterline, was visually found to be in fair condition, with fair painting work and light indentations observed at places. The port side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible.</p><p><br></p><p>During our attendance, the visible condition of the deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop decks was found to be in fair condition. The port and starboard bulwark and railing on the forecastle, main, poop, and accommodation decks were found to be in order. However, the starboard side stairway on the main deck leading to the poop deck was found to be chipped and corroded.</p><p><br></p><p>The mooring equipment, including double bollards, fairleads, and rollers, was found to be in fair condition, but the Safe Working Load (SWL) information was not marked. Most air vents and sounding pipes were also not clearly marked with the compartments they serve. The watertight door leading to the engine room was found to be corroded on the lower part.</p><p><br></p><p>The winch and windlass were generally in fair condition and good operational order. The mooring work area was not slippery, with no signs of oil puddles or leaks on the forecastle deck.</p><p><br></p><p>At the time of our survey, approximately 80% of the cargo remained onboard. Therefore, we could not ascertain the cargo hold conditions or carry out a suction test on the bilges. Additionally, the ballast tank inspection could not be conducted since the manhole located on the tank top was covered by the remaining cargo at the time of our survey.</p><p><br></p><p>In the meantime, an inspection of the hatch covers and panels found them to be in order, with sufficient hatch cover plate thickness.</p><p><br></p><p>BRIDGE AND NAVIGATION</p><p><br></p><p>The navigation and communication equipment were in good condition when tested and considered sufficient for domestic or local trading within Indonesian waters.</p><p><br></p><p>The updated NTM (Indonesian Notice to Mariners) was available onboard. The nautical publications and charts were correctly updated, and the deck logbook was properly filled out by the crew. However, the stability booklet was not available onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>The changeover instructions for emergency steering and blackout procedures were available and posted on the bridge and engine room.</p><p><br></p><p>MACHINERY SPACE AND STEERING GEAR</p><p><br></p><p>The vessel has 1 (one) main engine, 2 (two) auxiliary engines located on the engine room as registered on the class certificate, and 2 (two) generator sets located on the boat deck. The Chief Engineer verbally advised that several machinery equipment testing records were available onboard. The Planned Maintenance System (PMS) was available onboard but not completed with the reminder of the pending items and the overdue maintenance record, as the vessel has just been handed over to the new owner. The engine manufacturer manual and service bulletins were available onboard and kept in the Engine Control Room (ECR).</p><p><br></p><p>The Chief Engineer verbally advised that the purifier had been disused from the vessel due to the vessel currently using an HSD (High-Speed Diesel) fuel engine. Insulation mats were not available on several switchboard panels. The engine logbook was inspected and found to be regularly updated.</p><p><br></p><p>Generally, the engine room, including the floor, was found to be oily with oil residue observed at places. The engine room bilges were found wet with oily water. The Chief Officer verbally advised that the engine crew was still carrying out maintenance on the shaft generator.</p><p><br></p><p>The emergency steering gear and generator were in good order when tested and were provided with emergency procedures. However, the test record was not available onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>SAFETYÂ </p><p><br></p><p>The safety equipment was found to be in order. A sufficient number of life jackets were provided onboard. 1 (one) lifeboat and 2 (two) life rafts were available on board. Portable fire extinguishers were in good condition and regularly serviced with sufficient numbers. The emergency fire pump test was available for our inspection and found to be in satisfactory condition.</p><p><br></p><p>The bunkering connection was found in good condition, and a save-all was installed in the surrounding area. However, most air vents and sounding pipes were not clearly marked with the compartments they serve.</p><p><br></p><p>== END ==</p>', '2024-05-13', '2025-05-13', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (32, 29, 3, 8, NULL, 'Survey', 'Completed', 'complete', NULL, NULL, '2024-06-05', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-05-06', '2025-05-06', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (33, 29, 3, 8, NULL, 'Survey', 'Completed', 'draft', NULL, NULL, '2024-06-05', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-05-06', '2025-05-06', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (34, 31, 3, 8, NULL, 'Survey', 'Completed', 'draft', NULL, NULL, '2024-06-01', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-05-02', '2025-05-02', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (35, 31, 3, 8, NULL, 'Survey', 'Completed', 'draft', NULL, NULL, '2024-06-01', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-05-02', '2025-05-02', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (36, 33, 1, 8, 'Junor Maulana Ahmad', 'Survey', 'Completed', 'complete', 'Tanjung Priok, Jakarta', '2024-05-07', '2024-05-29', '<p>The stability booklet and stability calculation made by C/O or the Master should be provided onboard the tanker. The BPI (Berita Pelaut Indonesia) and the charts should be provided with the latest update.<br></p>', '<p>The tanker''s structural condition was generally in fair condition, with fair paintwork and spot indentation found in some places. The draft marks on the port and starboard sides, as well as the load lines, were clearly marked. The deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop decks were found to be in fair condition. The emergency fire hose test was available, and found in good condition.</p><p>The navigation and communication equipment was sufficient for domestic local trading in Indonesian waters. During our inspection, we found that the machinery equipment was in good working order.</p><p><br></p><p>Nonetheless, the crew''s ability to work and operate the tanker is considered sufficient, and they were familiar with the type and size of the tanker.</p>', '<p>SUMMARY</p><p><br></p><p>The survey onboard MT "FIGAR JAYA I" was carried out on 08 May 2024 at Tanjung Priok, Jakarta, with attendance:</p><p><br></p><p>1.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>M. Edi Purwanto - Master of the vessel</p><p>2.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Ibrahim Malik - Chief Officer of the vessel</p><p>3.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Sainal ArifinÂ  - Second Officer of the vessel</p><p>4.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Ali TaufanÂ  - Chief Engineer of the vessel</p><p>5.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Suryanto Herson- Second Engineer of the vessel</p><p>6.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>VIncensius Leonardus - Third Engineer of the vessel</p><p><br></p><p>MT âFIGAR JAYA Iâ is a 584 GT tanker presently owned and operated by PT. Figar Jaya Samudra, Jakarta. The tanker has been lying anchored at Tanjung Priok Anchorage while waiting for the bunker instruction from the shore office. Furthermore, the tankerâs trading area is mostly in Tanjung Priok waters.</p><p><br></p><p>VESSEL CERTIFICATION AND DOCUMENTATION</p><p><br></p><p>Class survey records for hull and machinery were made available at the time of the survey. The class is maintained. In the meantime, the statutory certificate was in valid condition and not overdue from the next inspection schedule or renewal.</p><p><br></p><p>The latest UT thickness measurement report was not available onboard the tanker. Later, based on the provided UT thickness measurement report from the shore office, the diminution level of the tankerâs steel structures was still below the class maximum allowable limit of 20%.</p><p><br></p><p>GENERAL CONDITION OF HULL AND DECK</p><p><br></p><p>As far as could be ascertained, the port and starboard side shell plating from aft to fore-ends, from sheer down to the waterline, was visually found in fair condition, and fair painting works with light indentation were sighted in places. The port and starboard side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible.</p><p><br></p><p>At our attendance, the visible condition of the deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop deck was found in fair condition. The port and starboard bulwark and railing on the forecastle, main, poop, and accommodation deck were found in order but the bulwark was found slightly dented.</p><p><br></p><p>The mooring equipment, which is double bollard, fairlead, and roller, was found in fair condition, and the safe working load information was clearly marked. Most air vents and sounding pipes were also clearly marked with the compartment they serve. The watertight door leading to the engine room were found in fair condition with a good rubber seal.</p><p><br></p><p>The winch and windlass are generally in fair condition and good operation. Therefore, the mooring work area was found clean and dry on the forecastle deck.</p><p><br></p><p>The cargo pipeline was found in good condition with no indications of leakage. The pipeline information was also well marked, for each loading and discharging cargo line. The booby hatch of all cargo tanks was found in good condition, including the rubber sealing.</p><p><br></p><p>During our attendance, the tanker was still carrying cargo onboard. Therefore, we were not able to ascertain the internal condition of the cargo tank no. 1S, 1P, 2S and 2P. The tanker was fitted with 6 (six) cargo tanks with a maximum capacity approx. 1,073.714 MT.</p><p><br></p><p>According to the Master''s explanation, the tanker is single hull and single bottom type, hence, she doesn''t have a ballast tank. He further explained that the fore peak tank is used as a conditional ballast tank when the tanker needs to maintain her stability.</p><p><br></p><p>BRIDGE AND NAVIGATION</p><p><br></p><p>The navigation and communication equipment were in good condition when tested and considered sufficient for domestic or local trading within Indonesian waters. The tanker was provided with nautical publications, and bridge management records and kept updated. However, the latest Notice to Mariners (NTM) and charts has not been provided onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>MACHINERY SPACE AND STEERING GEAR</p><p><br></p><p>The tanker is provided with 1 (one) main engine, 2 (two) auxiliary engines and 1 (one) emergency generator, which were found to be in good running condition when tested at the time of our attendance. The engine logbook was sighted and found to be regularly updated.Â </p><p>Meanwhile, the Planned Maintenance System (PMS) and the engine manufacturer manual were available onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>The Chief Engineer verbally advised that the vessel was not using the purifier. The emergency steering gear and generator were provided with emergency procedures and were in order when tested.</p><p><br></p><p>SAFETYÂ </p><p><br></p><p>The safety equipment was in order. Sufficient numbers of life jackets were provided onboard. 1 (one) unit of lifeboat and 2 (two) units of life rafts were available on board. Portable fire extinguishers were in good condition and regularly serviced. The emergency fire pump test was available for our inspection and found in satisfactory condition.</p><p><br></p><p>The bunkering line, and cargo overboard loading and discharge were in good condition, complete with portable save-alls that were well fitted. The scupper plugs were also available onboard. The oily water separator was in order, with the overboard discharge valve properly secured and in the closed position.</p><p>== END ==</p>', '2024-04-29', '2025-04-29', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
INSERT INTO surveys (id, vessel_id, surveyor_id, broker_id, surveyor_name, condition_type, status, form_status, location, date_of_survey, due_date, concern_from_surveyor, resume_from_surveyor, summary, policy_period_start, policy_period_end, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (37, 33, 1, 8, 'Junor Maulana Ahmad', 'Survey', 'Completed', 'complete', 'Tanjung Priok, Jakarta', '2024-05-07', '2024-05-29', '<p>-</p>', '<p>-</p>', '<p>SUMMARY</p><p><br></p><p>The survey onboard MT "FIGAR JAYA I" was carried out on 08 May 2024 at Tanjung Priok, Jakarta, with attendance:</p><p><br></p><p>1.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>M. Edi Purwanto - Master of the vessel</p><p>2.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Ibrahim Malik - Chief Officer of the vessel</p><p>3.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Sainal ArifinÂ  - Second Officer of the vessel</p><p>4.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Ali TaufanÂ  - Chief Engineer of the vessel</p><p>5.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>Suryanto Herson- Second Engineer of the vessel</p><p>6.<span style="white-space:pre">	</span>VIncensius Leonardus - Third Engineer of the vessel</p><p><br></p><p>MT âFIGAR JAYA Iâ is a 584 GT tanker presently owned and operated by PT. Figar Jaya Samudra, Jakarta. The tanker has been lying anchored at Tanjung Priok Anchorage while waiting for the bunker instruction from the shore office. Furthermore, the tankerâs trading area is mostly in Tanjung Priok waters.</p><p><br></p><p>VESSEL CERTIFICATION AND DOCUMENTATION</p><p><br></p><p>Class survey records for hull and machinery were made available at the time of the survey. The class is maintained. In the meantime, the statutory certificate was in valid condition and not overdue from the next inspection schedule or renewal.</p><p><br></p><p>The latest UT thickness measurement report was not available onboard the tanker. Later, based on the provided UT thickness measurement report from the shore office, the diminution level of the tankerâs steel structures was still below the class maximum allowable limit of 20%.</p><p><br></p><p>GENERAL CONDITION OF HULL AND DECK</p><p><br></p><p>As far as could be ascertained, the port and starboard side shell plating from aft to fore-ends, from sheer down to the waterline, was visually found in fair condition, and fair painting works with light indentation were sighted at places. The port and starboard side draft mark and load lines were clearly marked and visible.</p><p><br></p><p>At our attendance, the visible condition of the deck plating on the forecastle, main, and poop deck was found in fair condition. The port and starboard bulwark and railing on the forecastle, main, poop, and accommodation deck were found in order but the bulwark was found slightly dented.</p><p><br></p><p>The mooring equipment, which are double bollard, fairlead, and roller, was found in fair condition, and the safe working load information was clearly marked. Most air vents and sounding pipes were also clearly marked with the compartment they serve. The watertight door leading to the engine room were found in fair condition with a good rubber seal. The booby hatch cover in way of fore peak store was found in order.</p><p><br></p><p>The winch and windlass are generally in fair condition and good operation. Therefore, the mooring work area was found clean and dry on the forecastle deck.</p><p><br></p><p>The cargo pipeline was found in good condition with no indications of leakage. The pipeline information was also well marked, for each loading and discharging cargo line. The booby hatch of all cargo tanks was found in good condition, including the rubber sealing.</p><p><br></p><p>During our attendance, the tanker was still carrying cargo onboard. Therefore, we were not able to ascertain the internal condition of the cargo tank no. 1S, 1P, 2S and 2P. The tanker was fitted with 6 (six) cargo tanks with a maximum capacity approx. 1,073.714 MT.</p><p><br></p><p>According to the Master''s explanation, the tanker is single hull and single bottom type, hence, she doesn''t have a ballast tank. He further explained that the fore peak tank is used as a conditional ballast tank when the tanker needs to maintain her stability.</p><p><br></p><p>BRIDGE AND NAVIGATION</p><p><br></p><p>The navigation and communication equipment were in good condition when tested and considered sufficient for domestic or local trading within Indonesian waters. The tanker was provided with nautical publications, and bridge management records and kept updated. However, the latest Notice to Mariners (NTM) and charts has not been provided onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>The changeover instructions for emergency steering procedure were available and posted on the bridge. The blackout procedure was not posted on bridge deck or engine room (ini kok gak masuk deficiency?).</p><p><br></p><p>MACHINERY SPACE AND STEERING GEAR</p><p><br></p><p>The tanker is provided with 1 (one) main engine, 2 (two) auxiliary engines and 1 (one) emergency generator, which were found to be in good running condition when tested at the time of our attendance. The engine logbook was sighted and found to be regularly updated. Meanwhile, the Planned Maintenance System (PMS) and the engine manufacturer manual was available onboard.</p><p><br></p><p>The Chief Engineer verbally advised that the vessel was not using the purifier. The emergency steering gear and generator were provided with emergency procedures and were in order when tested.</p><p><br></p><p>The emergency steering gear and generator were in good order when tested and provided with emergency procedures. The test record was also available onboard.Â </p><p><br></p><p>SAFETY AND OIL POLLUTION PREVENTION</p><p><br></p><p>The safety equipment was in order. Sufficient numbers of life jackets were provided onboard. 1 (one) unit of lifeboat and 2 (two) units of life rafts were available on board. Portable fire extinguishers were in good condition and regularly serviced. The emergency fire pump test was available for our inspection and found in satisfactory condition.</p><p><br></p><p>The tanker was provided with a SOPEP box or locker complete with a SOPEP manual book. The oil absorbents were not provided on board. The pollution prevention equipment was sufficient in accordance with safety regulations.</p><p><br></p><p>The bunkering line, and cargo overboard loading and discharge were in good condition, complete with portable save-alls that were well fitted. The scupper plugs were also available onboard. The oily water separator was in order, with the overboard discharge valve properly secured and in the closed position.</p><p>== END ==</p>', '2024-04-29', '2025-04-29', NOW(), NOW()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, summary = EXCLUDED.summary;
